window.TR_M2={"id":"mlf-m02","title":"From Predictions to Probabilities","subtitle":"Probability, evidence, and probabilistic modeling","story":{"student":"Ava","features":[2,3],"weights":[10,5],"bias":20,"point_prediction":55,"conditional_counts":[[36,9],[22,33]],"assumed_std":10},"lessons":[{"id":"why","name":"Why probability?","short":"Outcomes, events, random variables","color":"#9E1B32"},{"id":"describe","name":"Describing uncertainty","short":"PMF, PDF, CDF and events","color":"#9E1B32"},{"id":"moments","name":"Expected value and variation","short":"Expectation, variance, standard deviation","color":"#9E1B32"},{"id":"conditional","name":"Probability from evidence","short":"Joint, marginal, conditional, independence","color":"#9E1B32"},{"id":"bayes","name":"Updating with Bayes","short":"Prior, likelihood, posterior","color":"#9E1B32"},{"id":"families","name":"Choosing a distribution","short":"Bernoulli, Binomial, categorical, Gaussian","color":"#9E1B32"},{"id":"learn","name":"Fitting probability models","short":"Likelihood, log-likelihood, MLE","color":"#9E1B32"},{"id":"predict","name":"Probabilistic prediction","short":"Conditional models, scoring and calibration","color":"#9E1B32"}],"slides":[{"section":"COURSE BRIDGE","title":"From a score to a range of possibilities","lead":"Module 1: Ava's features [2,3] produce the toy prediction 55. Module 2: how uncertain is that score?","math":"2 × 10 + 3 × 5 + 20 = 55 points","visual":"bridge","check":"Why is 55 not the same as P(pass)?","note":"Explicitly connect both modules. Predictions are point estimates; probabilities require assumptions about the possible outcomes.","code":"import torch\n# A point prediction does not specify uncertainty.\nx=torch.tensor([2.,3.]); w=torch.tensor([10.,5.])\nmu=x@w+20\nd=torch.distributions.Normal(mu,torch.tensor(10.))\nprint(\"Mean:\",mu.item(),\"P(score > 60):\",(1-d.cdf(torch.tensor(60.))).item())","checkAnswer":"55 is a predicted score in points. A pass probability is a number from 0 to 1. To calculate it, specify a passing threshold and the distribution of scores around 55.","explain":"A score is a number of points. A probability is a fraction between 0 and 1. To see the difference, count how often similar students clear the passing threshold."},{"section":"RECAP · MODULE 01","title":"What can we already do with tensors?","lead":"Represent observations; multiply a feature vector by weights; add a bias; compute predictions for a class.","math":"[2, 3] means [hours, sets]. Each new student adds one row.","visual":[["Ava","[2, 3]","hours, sets"],["Her prediction","55","2 × 10 + 3 × 5 + 20"],["A class","one row each","two columns per row"]],"check":"What does the first axis of X count?","note":"Ask students to reconstruct Ava's computation rather than merely naming matrix dimensions.","code":"import torch\nx = torch.tensor([2.,3.]); w=torch.tensor([10.,5.]); b=20.\nprint(x @ w + b)  # 55","checkAnswer":"The first axis counts examples. In the Study Coach matrix each row belongs to one student.","explain":"A feature is one measured input. A vector stores one student’s features in order. The matrix X stacks those vectors as rows."},{"section":"WHAT CHANGES THIS MODULE","title":"A prediction is not a promise","lead":"Suppose four students with the same study record scored 50, 60, 65 and 70. Three reached 60. A single predicted number cannot tell us how many passed.","math":"3 passes among 4 examples gives 3/4 = 0.75","visual":[["Threshold","60","include 60 when counting passes"],["Passing scores","60, 65, 70","three of four"],["Observed fraction","0.75","75%"]],"check":"What additional question can a distribution answer?","note":"Keep the example hypothetical: never treat toy study measurements as evidence of real student performance.","code":"import torch\nmu=torch.tensor(55.)\nnarrow=torch.distributions.Normal(mu,5.)\nwide=torch.distributions.Normal(mu,15.)\nprint(\"Different spreads:\",narrow.variance.item(),wide.variance.item())","checkAnswer":"For example, it can answer “How likely is Ava to score above 60?” by giving the area above 60 under the score density.","explain":"The count 3/4 describes these four examples. To forecast an unseen score, we need assumptions about what other scores could occur and how frequently."},{"section":"01 · WHY PROBABILITY?","title":"A second question about Ava","lead":"Ava has the same measurements as in Module 1: two study hours and three practice sets.","math":"x=[2,3]  →  f(x)=10(2)+5(3)+20=55","explain":"A point prediction summarizes a rule; it does not describe every possible exam score.","visual":"bridge","check":"If Ava's score is 55, must every equally active student score 55?","code":"import torch\nx=torch.tensor([2.,3.]); w=torch.tensor([10.,5.]); b=20.\nprint(\"Ava's toy score:\",(x@w+b).item())","note":"Open lesson 1 by asking: If Ava's score is 55, must every equally active student score 55?","lesson":"why","checkAnswer":"No. Students with the same recorded inputs can have different outcomes. The model assigns them the same predicted mean."},{"section":"01 · WHY PROBABILITY?","title":"What is uncertain?","lead":"We know Ava's study activity. We do not know her eventual exam result.","math":"Y = student's exam score, a random variable","explain":"Different outcomes can occur even when recorded features agree.","visual":"outcomes","check":"What does Y represent before the exam?","code":"import torch\nd=torch.distributions.Normal(55.,10.)\nprint(\"Five possible exam outcomes:\",d.sample((5,)))","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. Different outcomes can occur even when recorded features agree.","lesson":"why","checkAnswer":"Y represents Ava’s exam score before its value is known. After the exam, y denotes the score we observed."},{"section":"01 · WHY PROBABILITY?","title":"Outcomes and sample space","lead":"An outcome is one possible score; a sample space collects the possible outcomes.","math":"Ω = {0,1,2,…,100}  (if scores are whole percentages)","explain":"Specify the possible outcomes before assigning probabilities.","visual":"sample","check":"Would Ω change if exam scores include decimals?","code":"import torch\noutcomes=torch.arange(101) # Scores 0 through 100, inclusive\nprint(\"Possible integer scores:\",outcomes.shape,outcomes[0].item(),outcomes[-1].item())","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. Specify the possible outcomes before assigning probabilities.","lesson":"why","checkAnswer":"Yes. If we record to tenths, Ω includes values such as 67.4. If we model scores as continuously measured, Ω can contain every value in an interval."},{"section":"01 · WHY PROBABILITY?","title":"Events answer questions","lead":"The event 'passes' groups outcomes instead of naming one score.","math":"A = {Y ≥ 60};  P(A)=P(Y≥60)","explain":"An event is a set of outcomes; a probability measures how much probability lies in it.","visual":"threshold","check":"Is Y=67 an outcome or an event?","code":"import torch\nscores=torch.tensor([42.,55.,61.,67.,80.])\npassing=scores>=60\nprint(\"Pass event for each outcome:\",passing.tolist())","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. An event is a set of outcomes; a probability measures how much probability lies in it.","lesson":"why","checkAnswer":"67 is a possible score. The statement “Y = 67” defines the event containing outcomes that produce that score."},{"section":"01 · WHY PROBABILITY?","title":"Observed frequency vs model probability","lead":"Count observed outcomes to estimate frequencies, then make assumptions to generalize.","math":"observed pass fraction = passes ÷ students observed","explain":"The denominator names the reference group. In a class of ten, seven passes gives 7/10 = 0.7. In another group you must count again.","visual":"frequency","check":"What might change if we sampled a different class?","code":"import torch\nobserved=torch.tensor([1.]*18+[0.]*42)\nprint(\"Observed passing frequency:\",observed.mean().item())","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. A small class sample is not automatically an accurate population model.","lesson":"why","checkAnswer":"The fraction who pass can change. Even two samples from the same population can contain different numbers of passing students."},{"section":"01 · WHY PROBABILITY?","title":"Write the first random variable in PyTorch","lead":"Tensor operations still apply; now we can sample possible outcomes.","math":"μ (mu) = 55 points, the center. σ (sigma) = 10 points, the assumed spread.","explain":"Read Y given x as “the exam score for a student with these inputs.” Normal names the symmetric bell shape. We choose its spread here. Later we calculate spread from example scores.","visual":"normal","check":"Why is σ not implied by the prediction 55?","code":"import torch\nscore_dist = torch.distributions.Normal(torch.tensor(55.), torch.tensor(10.))\nprint(score_dist.sample((5,)))","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. The distribution parameters are tensors; the model adds assumptions about variation.","lesson":"why","checkAnswer":"55 locates the center. It gives no measure of spread. We must assume σ or estimate it from observed variation around predictions."},{"section":"02 · DESCRIBING UNCERTAINTY","title":"How can a distribution answer a question?","lead":"A probability distribution tells us how probability is allocated to possible outcomes.","math":"P(fail) + P(pass) = 1","explain":"Give all possible categories their shares of the total. A shorthand for “add every share” is Σ, the capital Greek letter sigma. For example, 0.3 + 0.7 covers the whole group.","visual":"distribution","check":"Which probabilities must add to one?","code":"import torch\noutcomes=torch.tensor([40.,55.,70.])\nprobs=torch.tensor([.2,.5,.3])\nprint(\"Discrete PMF sums to:\",probs.sum().item())","note":"Open lesson 2 by asking: Which probabilities must add to one?","lesson":"describe","checkAnswer":"Probabilities for a complete set of mutually exclusive outcomes sum to 1. For pass/fail, P(pass) + P(fail) = 1."},{"section":"02 · DESCRIBING UNCERTAINTY","title":"Discrete outcomes: a pass/fail coach","lead":"Represent passing as 1 and not passing as 0.","math":"Y∈{0,1};  P(Y=1)=p,  P(Y=0)=1−p","explain":"The probability mass function assigns probability to each possible value.","visual":"binary","check":"If p=0.7, what is P(Y=0)?","code":"import torch\nd=torch.distributions.Bernoulli(probs=torch.tensor(.7))\nprint(\"P(pass):\",d.log_prob(torch.tensor(1.)).exp().item())\nprint(\"P(fail):\",d.log_prob(torch.tensor(0.)).exp().item())","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. The probability mass function assigns probability to each possible value.","lesson":"describe","checkAnswer":"P(Y = 0) = 1 − 0.7 = 0.3. The two outcomes exhaust the possibilities."},{"section":"02 · DESCRIBING UNCERTAINTY","title":"Continuous outcomes need density","lead":"A continuous score model spreads probability over intervals.","math":"Probability of a score in a band = area under the density over that band.","explain":"Imagine 20 of 100 scores land in a band 10 points wide. A rectangle with height 0.02 per point and width 10 has area 0.20. The smooth curve uses the same area idea with narrower strips.","visual":"density","check":"Why does P(Y=55)=0 for an ideal continuous model?","code":"import torch\nd=torch.distributions.Normal(55.,10.)\nprint(\"PDF height at 60:\",d.log_prob(torch.tensor(60.)).exp().item())\nprint(\"P(50<=Y<=60):\",(d.cdf(torch.tensor(60.))-d.cdf(torch.tensor(50.))).item())","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. The PDF is a density: its height is not the probability of one exact score.","lesson":"describe","checkAnswer":"A single point has zero width, so its area under a continuous density is zero, even when the density height there is positive."},{"section":"02 · DESCRIBING UNCERTAINTY","title":"Areas answer interval questions","lead":"Students care about passing or scoring within a band.","math":"P(50 ≤ Y ≤ 60) = area from 50 to 60. The symbol ∫ means accumulate tiny strips of area.","explain":"For scores above 60, start at 60 and shade toward the right. For a bounded interval, stop at its upper end. You can read the shaded area without doing integral calculus.","visual":"interval","check":"Which area corresponds to scores above 60?","code":"import torch\nd=torch.distributions.Normal(55.,10.)\nlower,upper=torch.tensor(50.),torch.tensor(60.)\nprint(\"Area P(50<=Y<=60):\",(d.cdf(upper)-d.cdf(lower)).item())","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. Probability comes from an area under the density curve.","lesson":"describe","checkAnswer":"Use the area under the density to the right of 60. Each small interval there contributes to P(Y > 60)."},{"section":"02 · DESCRIBING UNCERTAINTY","title":"The cumulative distribution function","lead":"The CDF accumulates probability from the left.","math":"F(t)=P(Y≤t);  P(Y>t)=1−F(t)","explain":"F stands for the fraction accumulated from the left. If 70% are at or below a cutoff, the remaining 30% lie above it. The CDF does this at every cutoff.","visual":"cdf","check":"How would you use F(60) to compute passing above 60?","code":"import torch\nd=torch.distributions.Normal(55.,10.)\nF60=d.cdf(torch.tensor(60.))\nprint(\"F(60):\",F60.item(),\"P(Y>60):\",(1-F60).item())","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. CDFs work for discrete and continuous random variables.","lesson":"describe","checkAnswer":"F(60) is P(Y ≤ 60). Subtract it from 1 to get P(Y > 60)."},{"section":"02 · DESCRIBING UNCERTAINTY","title":"Ask PyTorch for the probability","lead":"Continue using tensors, now with distributions and their CDFs.","math":"Y|x ~ Normal(55,10²);  P(Y>60)≈0.3085","explain":"This probability follows from the assumed normal model.","visual":"threshold","check":"Would this tail probability increase or decrease as σ grows?","code":"import torch\nd = torch.distributions.Normal(55., 10.)\np_above_60 = 1 - d.cdf(torch.tensor(60.))\nprint(round(p_above_60.item(), 4))  # 0.3085","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. This probability follows from the assumed normal model.","lesson":"describe","checkAnswer":"It increases for this threshold above the fixed mean of 55. As σ grows, 60 lies fewer standard deviations above the mean, leaving more area to its right."},{"section":"03 · EXPECTED VALUE AND VARIATION","title":"Averages are useful—but incomplete","lead":"Two classes can have the same mean score and different spreads.","math":"E[Y] = μ","explain":"The mean summarizes the center; it cannot tell us everything.","visual":"means","check":"What information is lost when we report only the average?","code":"import torch\na=torch.distributions.Normal(55.,5.)\nb=torch.distributions.Normal(55.,15.)\nprint(\"Means:\",a.mean.item(),b.mean.item())\nprint(\"Standard deviations:\",a.stddev.item(),b.stddev.item())","note":"Open lesson 3 by asking: What information is lost when we report only the average?","lesson":"moments","checkAnswer":"The average hides how far scores spread and how the distribution is shaped. Classes with identical means can have very different chances of exceeding 60."},{"section":"03 · EXPECTED VALUE AND VARIATION","title":"Expected value is probability-weighted","lead":"For a discrete variable, multiply each outcome by its probability.","math":"E[Y]=Σᵧ yP(Y=y)","explain":"If three of four equally likely scores are 60 and the other is 40, the mean is (60 + 60 + 60 + 40)/4 = 55. Weighting 60 by ¾ and 40 by ¼ gives the same answer.","visual":"weighted","check":"Compute E[Y] if 50% score 40 and 50% score 60.","code":"import torch\nvalues=torch.tensor([40.,60.]); p=torch.tensor([.5,.5])\nprint(\"Expected score:\",(values*p).sum().item())","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. Expectation is a weighted average over possible outcomes.","lesson":"moments","checkAnswer":"E[Y] = 0.5 × 40 + 0.5 × 60 = 50 points."},{"section":"03 · EXPECTED VALUE AND VARIATION","title":"Variance measures squared spread","lead":"Measure deviations from the mean and average their squares.","math":"Var(Y)=E[(Y−μ)²]","explain":"For equally likely scores 40 and 60, the mean is 50. The misses are −10 and +10. Squaring gives 100 and 100. Their average is 100 squared points; its square root, 10 points, is the standard deviation.","visual":"variance","check":"Why would using raw deviations give zero?","code":"import torch\nvalues=torch.tensor([40.,60.]); p=torch.tensor([.5,.5])\nmu=(values*p).sum()\nprint(\"Variance:\",((values-mu).square()*p).sum().item())","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. Squaring makes negative and positive deviations contribute equally.","lesson":"moments","checkAnswer":"Signed deviations from the mean cancel. For example, 40 and 60 deviate from 50 by −10 and +10. Squaring makes both contributions positive."},{"section":"03 · EXPECTED VALUE AND VARIATION","title":"Standard deviation returns to score units","lead":"Take a square root to express spread in original units.","math":"σ=√Var(Y)","explain":"If exam scores are points, σ is in points rather than squared points.","visual":"spread","check":"Compare two models with σ=5 and σ=15.","code":"import torch\nvalues=torch.tensor([40.,60.]); p=torch.tensor([.5,.5])\nmu=(values*p).sum()\nvariance=((values-mu).square()*p).sum()\nprint(\"Std deviation in score points:\",variance.sqrt().item())","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. If exam scores are points, σ is in points rather than squared points.","lesson":"moments","checkAnswer":"With the same mean, σ = 15 has three times the standard deviation and nine times the variance of σ = 5. Its scores spread farther from the mean."},{"section":"03 · EXPECTED VALUE AND VARIATION","title":"Same mean; different uncertainty","lead":"A model centered at 55 may be narrow or broad.","math":"Normal(55,5²) vs Normal(55,15²)","explain":"Use the Lab to widen the distribution without changing its mean.","visual":"normal","check":"Which model predicts scores between 50 and 60 more confidently?","code":"import torch\nfor sigma in [5.,15.]:\n    d=torch.distributions.Normal(55.,sigma)\n    print(\"Sigma:\",sigma,\"P(score > 60):\",(1-d.cdf(torch.tensor(60.))).item())","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. Use the Lab to widen the distribution without changing its mean.","lesson":"moments","checkAnswer":"The σ = 5 model puts more probability between 50 and 60. Around a mean of 55, this interval spans ±1 standard deviation for σ = 5 and only ±⅓ for σ = 15."},{"section":"03 · EXPECTED VALUE AND VARIATION","title":"Sampling approximates expectation","lead":"Compute a sample average of draws from the distribution.","math":"Ȳₙ=(1/n)Σᵢ Yᵢ → μ as n grows (under standard assumptions)","explain":"Repeated draws reveal how Monte Carlo estimates stabilize.","visual":"sampling","check":"Why is n=5 generally less stable than n=1000?","code":"import torch\ntorch.manual_seed(7)\nd = torch.distributions.Normal(55., 10.)\nvalues = d.sample((1000,))\nprint(values.mean().item(), values.std(unbiased=False).item())","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. Repeated draws reveal how Monte Carlo estimates stabilize.","lesson":"moments","checkAnswer":"An unusual draw has more influence in a five-draw average. For independent draws with common variance, the standard error of the mean is σ/√n."},{"section":"04 · PROBABILITY FROM EVIDENCE","title":"One table, three probability questions","lead":"Did students who completed more practice sets pass more often?","math":"100 illustrative students: rows=practice group; columns=pass","explain":"The same data supports different probabilities, depending on the question.","visual":"table","check":"Which denominator changes if we condition on practice?","code":"import torch\ncounts=torch.tensor([[36.,9.],[22.,33.]])\nprint(\"Row totals:\",counts.sum(dim=1))\nprint(\"Column totals:\",counts.sum(dim=0))\nprint(\"Overall total:\",counts.sum().item())","note":"Open lesson 4 by asking: Which denominator changes if we condition on practice?","lesson":"conditional","checkAnswer":"Conditioning on at least three practice sets restricts the reference group to the 45 students in that row. The denominator changes from 100 to 45."},{"section":"04 · PROBABILITY FROM EVIDENCE","title":"Calculate a joint probability","lead":"A joint event requires both conditions at once.","math":"P(Pass ∩ Practice≥3)=36/100=0.36","explain":"Joint probability uses the entire cohort as the denominator.","visual":"table","check":"What does the number 36 count?","code":"import torch\ncounts=torch.tensor([[36.,9.],[22.,33.]])\nprint(\"P(pass AND practice >=3):\",(counts[0,0]/counts.sum()).item())","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. Joint probability uses the entire cohort as the denominator.","lesson":"conditional","checkAnswer":"36 students both completed at least three sets and passed. The joint probability is 36/100 = 0.36."},{"section":"04 · PROBABILITY FROM EVIDENCE","title":"Calculate the marginal","lead":"Ignore practice group, then count all students who passed.","math":"P(Pass)=(36+22)/100=0.58","explain":"Marginalization sums across the values of another variable.","visual":"table","check":"What is P(Practice≥3)?","code":"import torch\ncounts=torch.tensor([[36.,9.],[22.,33.]])\nprint(\"P(pass):\",(counts[:,0].sum()/counts.sum()).item())","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. Marginalization sums across the values of another variable.","lesson":"conditional","checkAnswer":"Add the two cells in the high-practice row. P(practice ≥ 3) = (36 + 9)/100 = 0.45."},{"section":"04 · PROBABILITY FROM EVIDENCE","title":"Now condition on observed evidence","lead":"Restrict to the 45 students who completed ≥3 sets.","math":"P(Pass | Practice≥3)=36/45=0.80","explain":"Conditional probability changes the reference group.","visual":"conditional","check":"Why is 36/100 wrong for this question?","code":"import torch\ncounts=torch.tensor([[36.,9.],[22.,33.]])\nprint(\"P(pass | practice>=3):\",(counts[0,0]/counts[0].sum()).item())","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. Conditional probability changes the reference group.","lesson":"conditional","checkAnswer":"36/100 describes the joint event in the entire class. Among the 45 high-practice students, the fraction who passed is 36/45 = 0.80."},{"section":"04 · PROBABILITY FROM EVIDENCE","title":"Do not reverse a conditional","lead":"P(Pass | Practice≥3) differs from P(Practice≥3 | Pass).","math":"P(Practice≥3 | Pass)=36/58≈0.621","explain":"Both are valid, but they answer different questions.","visual":"conditional","check":"Which denominator describes students who passed?","code":"import torch\ncounts=torch.tensor([[36.,9.],[22.,33.]])\nprint(\"P(practice | pass):\",(counts[0,0]/counts[:,0].sum()).item())\nprint(\"P(pass | practice):\",(counts[0,0]/counts[0].sum()).item())","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. Both are valid, but they answer different questions.","lesson":"conditional","checkAnswer":"Start with the 58 students who passed (36 + 22). Among them, 36 practiced at least three sets, giving 36/58 ≈ 0.621."},{"section":"04 · PROBABILITY FROM EVIDENCE","title":"Test an independence claim","lead":"Independence means conditioning does not change the probability.","math":"If independent: P(A|B)=P(A)","explain":"Here 0.80 differs from 0.58, so these events are not independent in the toy dataset.","visual":"independence","check":"Does this association alone show practice causes passing?","code":"import torch\ncounts = torch.tensor([[36., 9.], [22., 33.]])\np_pass = counts[:,0].sum() / counts.sum()\np_pass_given_practice = counts[0,0] / counts[0,:].sum()\nprint(p_pass.item(), p_pass_given_practice.item())","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. Here 0.80 differs from 0.58, so these events are not independent in the toy dataset.","lesson":"conditional","checkAnswer":"No. For instance, prior preparation might influence both practice habits and exam performance. The table alone cannot isolate the effect of practice."},{"section":"05 · UPDATING WITH BAYES","title":"A new practice-test result arrives","lead":"Start a fresh example of 100 students. Suppose 60 eventually pass and 40 fail. We will use this group to interpret a practice-test signal.","math":"Prior P(Pass)=0.60","explain":"Before seeing a practice result, use the group pass fraction, 60/100. This starting probability is called a prior.","visual":[["Starting group","100","fresh example"],["Eventually pass","60","prior 0.60"],["Eventually fail","40","prior 0.40"]],"check":"Should evidence be ignored once we have an initial prediction?","code":"import torch\nprior=torch.tensor([.6,.4]) # pass, fail\nprint(\"Prior probability vector:\",prior,\"Sum:\",prior.sum().item())","note":"Open lesson 5 by asking: Should evidence be ignored once we have an initial prediction?","lesson":"bayes","checkAnswer":"Use the new test result to update the prior. A signal more common among passing students increases the pass probability."},{"section":"05 · UPDATING WITH BAYES","title":"A likelihood describes evidence","lead":"How frequently is a strong practice-test result observed under each outcome?","math":"P(Strong | Pass)=0.80;  P(Strong | Fail)=0.25","explain":"Read the vertical bar as “among.” A strong signal occurs for 48 of the 60 eventual passes and 10 of the 40 eventual failures. These fractions describe two different groups.","visual":[["Among 60 passes","48 strong","48/60 = 0.80"],["Among 40 failures","10 strong","10/40 = 0.25"]],"check":"Why is P(Strong|Pass) not P(Pass|Strong)?","code":"import torch\nlikelihood=torch.tensor([.8,.25]) # strong test | pass,fail\nprint(\"P(strong | pass), P(strong | fail):\",likelihood)\nprint(\"Weak result likelihoods:\",1-likelihood)","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. The likelihood is about evidence given a hypothesis, not the reverse.","lesson":"bayes","checkAnswer":"P(Strong | Pass) starts with passing students. P(Pass | Strong) starts with all students who received a strong result. Their denominators differ."},{"section":"05 · UPDATING WITH BAYES","title":"Build the evidence denominator","lead":"The same strong signal may occur in passing and failing groups.","math":"P(Strong)=0.80(0.60)+0.25(0.40)=0.58","explain":"Count all the strong results: 48 + 10 = 58. Dividing by 100 gives 0.58. The multiplication below reproduces those counts as shares of the original group.","visual":[["Pass and strong","48","0.80 × 60"],["Fail and strong","10","0.25 × 40"],["All strong","58","new reference group"]],"check":"Why must we include the failing group?","code":"import torch\nprior=torch.tensor([.6,.4])\nlikelihood=torch.tensor([.8,.25])\nprint(\"P(strong):\",(prior*likelihood).sum().item())","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. Total probability combines the alternative explanations for the evidence.","lesson":"bayes","checkAnswer":"Some students who fail also produce a strong signal. Their contribution, 0.25 × 0.40, must be included in the total probability of that signal."},{"section":"05 · UPDATING WITH BAYES","title":"Compute the posterior","lead":"Update the passing probability after a strong practice-test result.","math":"P(Pass|Strong)=0.48/0.58≈0.828","explain":"Within the 58 strong results, 48 belong to students who passed. The updated probability, called the posterior, is 48/58. This count is Bayes’ rule written in people.","visual":[["Before seeing signal","60 / 100","all students"],["After strong signal","48 / 58","strong results only"]],"check":"What if the signal were equally likely in both groups?","code":"import torch\nprior=torch.tensor([.6,.4]); strong=torch.tensor([.8,.25])\nweights=prior*strong\nprint(\"P(pass | strong):\",(weights/weights.sum())[0].item())","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. The signal raises the estimate because it is more likely among students who pass.","lesson":"bayes","checkAnswer":"For a signal with the same positive likelihood in both groups, the posterior equals the prior. The signal gives no reason to prefer either group."},{"section":"05 · UPDATING WITH BAYES","title":"Weak evidence can reverse the direction","lead":"A weak test result also contains information.","math":"P(Pass|Weak)=0.20(0.60)/[0.20(0.60)+0.75(0.40)]≈0.286","explain":"Of the 42 weak results, 12 belong to students who passed and 30 to students who failed. The passing fraction among weak results is 12/42, about 0.286.","visual":"posterior","check":"Compare the two posterior probabilities.","code":"import torch\nprior=torch.tensor([.6,.4]); weak=torch.tensor([.2,.75])\nweights=prior*weak\nprint(\"P(pass | weak):\",(weights/weights.sum())[0].item())","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. The prior remains the same; the observed signal changes the posterior.","lesson":"bayes","checkAnswer":"A strong result raises the pass probability from 60% to about 82.8%. A weak result lowers it to about 28.6%."},{"section":"05 · UPDATING WITH BAYES","title":"Implement a Bayes update with tensors","lead":"Multiplication and normalization connect probability to Module 1 operations.","math":"posterior ∝ likelihood × prior","explain":"Vectorize hypotheses, multiply by evidence likelihoods, normalize.","visual":"bayes","check":"Which tensor operation makes probabilities sum to one?","code":"import torch\nprior = torch.tensor([0.60, 0.40])  # pass, fail\nlikelihood = torch.tensor([0.80, 0.25])  # strong test\nweights = prior * likelihood\nposterior = weights / weights.sum()\nprint(posterior)  # tensor([0.8276, 0.1724])","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. Vectorize hypotheses, multiply by evidence likelihoods, normalize.","lesson":"bayes","checkAnswer":"Multiply prior and likelihood elementwise, then divide by the sum of the resulting weights. The normalized entries sum to 1."},{"section":"06 · CHOOSING A DISTRIBUTION","title":"Choose the model from the outcome","lead":"A probability model must match what can actually happen.","math":"Binary ≠ count ≠ category ≠ continuous score","explain":"Distribution choice starts with the meaning and support of Y.","visual":"families","check":"Can a Gaussian represent exact pass/fail labels without a link?","code":"import torch\nmodels={\n  \"Bernoulli\":torch.distributions.Bernoulli(probs=.8),\n  \"Binomial\":torch.distributions.Binomial(total_count=10,probs=.8),\n  \"Categorical\":torch.distributions.Categorical(probs=torch.tensor([.2,.5,.3])),\n  \"Normal\":torch.distributions.Normal(55.,10.)\n}\nfor name,d in models.items(): print(name,\"mean:\",d.mean)","note":"Open lesson 6 by asking: Can a Gaussian represent exact pass/fail labels without a link?","lesson":"families","checkAnswer":"Use a Bernoulli model for a single pass/fail label. A Gaussian assigns density across a continuum and gives zero probability to either exact point."},{"section":"06 · CHOOSING A DISTRIBUTION","title":"Bernoulli: one yes/no result","lead":"Model whether one student passes an assessment.","math":"Y~Bernoulli(p)","explain":"The only possible values are 0 and 1.","visual":"binary","check":"What is the expected value of a Bernoulli variable?","code":"import torch\nd=torch.distributions.Bernoulli(probs=.8)\nprint(\"Expected pass indicator:\",d.mean.item())\nprint(\"P(fail):\",d.log_prob(torch.tensor(0.)).exp().item())","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. The only possible values are 0 and 1.","lesson":"families","checkAnswer":"For a 0/1 variable, E[Y] = 1 × p + 0 × (1 − p) = p."},{"section":"06 · CHOOSING A DISTRIBUTION","title":"Binomial: how many successes?","lead":"Count the number of students who pass in n comparable trials.","math":"K~Binomial(n,p)","explain":"Independence and a shared success probability are modeling assumptions.","visual":"binomial","check":"Why are 10 separate students different from 10 score categories?","code":"import torch\nd=torch.distributions.Binomial(total_count=10,probs=.8)\nprint(\"Expected passes among 10:\",d.mean.item())\nprint(\"P(exactly 8 passes):\",d.log_prob(torch.tensor(8.)).exp().item())","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. Independence and a shared success probability are modeling assumptions.","lesson":"families","checkAnswer":"Ten students give ten trials whose successes we can count. Ten categories give ten alternatives for one observation."},{"section":"06 · CHOOSING A DISTRIBUTION","title":"Categorical: more than two classes","lead":"Predict one of several performance bands.","math":"Y∈{low,medium,high}; Σₖpₖ=1","explain":"Each category gets probability mass; labels are not ordinary score measurements.","visual":"categories","check":"Why must class probabilities sum to 1?","code":"import torch\nd=torch.distributions.Categorical(probs=torch.tensor([.2,.5,.3]))\nprint(\"P(low,medium,high):\",d.probs)\nprint(\"P(medium):\",d.log_prob(torch.tensor(1)).exp().item())","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. Each category gets probability mass; labels are not ordinary score measurements.","lesson":"families","checkAnswer":"Each observation receives exactly one class. Adding probabilities over all classes therefore gives the probability of any possible class, which is 1."},{"section":"06 · CHOOSING A DISTRIBUTION","title":"Gaussian: a numerical measurement","lead":"Represent a continuous score around a mean with a spread.","math":"Y|x~Normal(μ(x),σ²)","explain":"The normal model is illustrative; actual bounded exam scores may require other models.","visual":"normal","check":"What happens if the distribution allows scores above 100?","code":"import torch\nd=torch.distributions.Normal(loc=torch.tensor(55.),scale=torch.tensor(10.))\nprint(\"Expected score:\",d.mean.item(),\"Variance:\",d.variance.item())\nprint(\"P(score>60):\",(1-d.cdf(torch.tensor(60.))).item())","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. The normal model is illustrative; actual bounded exam scores may require other models.","lesson":"families","checkAnswer":"An ordinary Gaussian gives some probability to impossible scores above 100. Check how large that probability is and use a bounded score model if it matters."},{"section":"06 · CHOOSING A DISTRIBUTION","title":"Parameters can be tensors","lead":"PyTorch creates Bernoulli, Categorical and Normal distributions from tensors.","math":"distribution parameters → sampling, log_prob, cdf","explain":"Revisit Module 1: tensor computation powers probabilistic modeling too.","visual":"families","check":"What is the difference between a tensor and a distribution?","code":"import torch\npass_flag = torch.distributions.Bernoulli(probs=torch.tensor(.8))\nscore = torch.distributions.Normal(loc=torch.tensor(55.), scale=torch.tensor(10.))\nprint(pass_flag.sample((5,)))\nprint(score.log_prob(torch.tensor(60.)).item())","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. Revisit Module 1: tensor computation powers probabilistic modeling too.","lesson":"families","checkAnswer":"A tensor stores numerical values. A distribution uses parameters, often tensors, to define how outcomes vary and to compute probabilities or densities."},{"section":"07 · FITTING PROBABILITY MODELS","title":"Data help choose distribution parameters","lead":"The distribution family is an assumption; observations inform its parameters.","math":"Data: 42, 48, 55, 61, 69","explain":"Parameter learning asks which distribution makes these measurements plausible.","visual":"data","check":"Which quantities are observed, and which do we estimate?","code":"import torch\nscores=torch.tensor([42.,48.,55.,61.,69.])\nprint(\"Observed scores:\",scores)\nprint(\"Their mean:\",scores.mean().item())","note":"Open lesson 7 by asking: Which quantities are observed, and which do we estimate?","lesson":"learn","checkAnswer":"The five scores (42, 48, 55, 61, 69) are observed. We can estimate the population mean μ and variance σ² from them."},{"section":"07 · FITTING PROBABILITY MODELS","title":"Likelihood evaluates parameter choices","lead":"Given observed scores, evaluate how their density changes as parameters change.","math":"L(θ)=∏ᵢ p(yᵢ|θ)","explain":"Imagine a biased coin and two observed heads on independent flips. A candidate heads probability of 0.8 gives 0.8 × 0.8 = 0.64; 0.5 gives 0.25. The mark ∏ means multiply the contributions. For scores we compare densities in the same way.","visual":[["Observed","H, H","two independent flips"],["Chance of heads 0.5","0.25","0.5 × 0.5"],["Chance of heads 0.8","0.64","0.8 × 0.8"]],"check":"Why do parameters change while data stay fixed?","code":"import torch\nscores=torch.tensor([42.,48.,55.,61.,69.])\nfor mu in [50.,55.,60.]:\n    d=torch.distributions.Normal(mu,10.)\n    print(\"Candidate mean:\",mu,\"Likelihood:\",d.log_prob(scores).sum().exp().item())","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. For continuous data, p here is a density evaluated at the observations.","lesson":"learn","checkAnswer":"The scores have already been observed. Likelihood asks which candidate parameter values assign those same observations greater joint density."},{"section":"07 · FITTING PROBABILITY MODELS","title":"Why take the logarithm?","lead":"Products of many small numbers can underflow; logarithms turn products into sums.","math":"log L(θ)=Σᵢ log p(yᵢ|θ)","explain":"A logarithm asks which exponent produced a number. For example, 10² = 100, so log₁₀(100) = 2. Natural logs use a different base, e ≈ 2.718. Multiplication becomes addition with either base: log(ab) = log(a) + log(b).","visual":[["Two factors","0.5 × 0.5","joint probability 0.25"],["Two log terms","ln(0.5) + ln(0.5)","same as ln(0.25)"]],"check":"Which operation replaces multiplication?","code":"import torch\nscores=torch.tensor([42.,48.,55.,61.,69.])\nd=torch.distributions.Normal(55.,10.)\nprint(\"Sum log-likelihood:\",d.log_prob(scores).sum().item())\nprint(\"Log of product:\",d.log_prob(scores).exp().prod().log().item())","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. Maximizing log-likelihood is equivalent to maximizing likelihood.","lesson":"learn","checkAnswer":"Taking logarithms turns a product of positive likelihood contributions into a sum of log-likelihood contributions."},{"section":"07 · FITTING PROBABILITY MODELS","title":"Estimate the Gaussian mean and variance","lead":"For the toy scores, the MLE mean is 55 and variance is 90.","math":"μ̂=(1/n)Σyᵢ=55;  σ̂²=(1/n)Σ(yᵢ−55)²=90","explain":"The 1/n variance is the MLE; the unbiased sample estimator uses 1/(n−1).","visual":"mle","check":"Why are there two common variance denominators?","code":"import torch\nscores=torch.tensor([42.,48.,55.,61.,69.])\nmu=scores.mean();var_mle=((scores-mu)**2).mean()\nprint(\"MLE mean:\",mu.item(),\"MLE variance:\",var_mle.item())","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. The 1/n variance is the MLE; the unbiased sample estimator uses 1/(n−1).","lesson":"learn","checkAnswer":"Dividing by n gives the Gaussian maximum-likelihood variance. With an estimated mean and independent, identically distributed observations, dividing by n − 1 corrects the downward bias in estimating population variance."},{"section":"07 · FITTING PROBABILITY MODELS","title":"Loss is negative log-likelihood","lead":"Convert a maximization into a familiar minimization.","math":"NLL(θ)=−Σᵢ log p(yᵢ|θ)","explain":"For an observed pass, a model assigning probability 0.8 has loss −ln(0.8) ≈ 0.223. A model assigning 0.2 has loss ≈ 1.609. Smaller loss rewards the more plausible observation. For a continuous exam score, evaluate its density in place of a category probability.","visual":[["True outcome","Pass","score its assigned probability"],["Assign 0.8","loss 0.223","more plausible"],["Assign 0.2","loss 1.609","less plausible"]],"check":"What parameters of a Normal model could we learn?","code":"import torch\nscores=torch.tensor([42.,48.,55.,61.,69.])\nd=torch.distributions.Normal(55.,10.)\nnll=-d.log_prob(scores).sum()\nprint(\"Negative log-likelihood:\",nll.item())","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. Module 1's gradient-based optimization can minimize this loss.","lesson":"learn","checkAnswer":"We can learn the mean μ and the positive standard deviation σ. For Ava’s first update we hold σ at 10 and learn the parameters that produce μ."},{"section":"07 · FITTING PROBABILITY MODELS","title":"Fit a distribution in PyTorch","lead":"Use torch.distributions and gradient-enabled parameters.","math":"argmin μ,logσ −Σ log Normal(yᵢ;μ,σ).pdf","explain":"The likelihood quantifies fit; it does not by itself prove calibration or correctness.","visual":"nll","check":"Why must σ stay positive?","code":"import torch\ny = torch.tensor([42., 48., 55., 61., 69.])\nmu = y.mean()\nvar_mle = ((y-mu)**2).mean()\nprint(mu.item(), var_mle.item())  # 55.0, 90.0","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. The likelihood quantifies fit; it does not by itself prove calibration or correctness.","lesson":"learn","checkAnswer":"A Normal distribution requires a positive scale. Zero gives no ordinary Gaussian density, and a negative value cannot represent standard deviation."},{"section":"08 · PROBABILISTIC PREDICTION","title":"Go back to Ava","lead":"A model can return a probability distribution conditional on the same input vector.","math":"x=[2,3];  μ(x)=xᵀw+b=55","explain":"Tensor computation supplies parameters; a distribution specifies possible outcomes.","visual":"bridge","check":"What does the input vector determine?","code":"import torch\nx=torch.tensor([2.,3.]);w=torch.tensor([10.,5.])\nmu=x@w+20\nd=torch.distributions.Normal(mu,10.)\nprint(\"Toy mean:\",mu.item(),\"Example outcomes:\",d.sample((3,)))","note":"Open lesson 8 by asking: What does the input vector determine?","lesson":"predict","checkAnswer":"With fixed weights and bias, the input determines the conditional mean μ(x). Here [2, 3] gives 55. The model specifies σ separately."},{"section":"08 · PROBABILISTIC PREDICTION","title":"Regression: predict a distribution","lead":"Instead of only a mean, return a mean and an assumed or learned spread.","math":"Y|X=x~Normal(μθ(x),σθ(x)²)","explain":"Use the CDF for threshold decisions and sampling for possible outcomes.","visual":"normal","check":"Why is one fixed σ a model choice, not a measured fact?","code":"import torch\nx=torch.tensor([2.,3.]);w=torch.tensor([10.,5.])\nmu=x@w+20; sigma=torch.tensor(10.)\nd=torch.distributions.Normal(mu,sigma)\nprint(\"P(score>60|x):\",(1-d.cdf(torch.tensor(60.))).item())","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. Use the CDF for threshold decisions and sampling for possible outcomes.","lesson":"predict","checkAnswer":"The mean prediction gives no information about how residuals vary across students. Using the same σ for everyone assumes a common spread."},{"section":"08 · PROBABILISTIC PREDICTION","title":"Classification: output pass probability","lead":"A logistic link maps any real-valued score to (0,1).","math":"pθ(x)=1/(1+exp(−(wᵀx+b)))","explain":"A binary label uses Bernoulli, not a Gaussian score distribution.","visual":"sigmoid","check":"Why can't we call a raw unbounded score a probability?","code":"import torch\nx=torch.tensor([2.,3.]);w=torch.tensor([-.6,.9]);b=-.5\np_pass=torch.sigmoid(x@w+b)\nprint(\"P(pass|x) via logistic link:\",p_pass.item())","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. A binary label uses Bernoulli, not a Gaussian score distribution.","lesson":"predict","checkAnswer":"A raw score can be negative or exceed 1. A sigmoid maps it into (0, 1), after which we can check how well the resulting probabilities match observed frequencies."},{"section":"08 · PROBABILISTIC PREDICTION","title":"Predictive evaluation asks two questions","lead":"Was the class prediction accurate? Were reported probabilities appropriate?","math":"NLL penalizes confident mistakes; calibration compares predictions to outcomes","explain":"Evaluate on held-out data, not only the training sample.","visual":"calibration","check":"What happens to log loss when p→0 for a true event?","code":"import torch\noutcomes=torch.tensor([1.,0.,1.,1.])\nprobs=torch.tensor([.8,.6,.7,.9])\nnll=torch.nn.functional.binary_cross_entropy(probs,outcomes)\nprint(\"Average negative log-likelihood:\",nll.item())","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. Evaluate on held-out data, not only the training sample.","lesson":"predict","checkAnswer":"For an event that occurred, log loss is −log(p). It grows without bound as p approaches zero."},{"section":"08 · PROBABILISTIC PREDICTION","title":"Uncertainty must be supported by evidence","lead":"A probability number is meaningful only relative to assumptions and validation.","math":"Model family + parameters + evidence + evaluation","explain":"Separate observed variability, imperfect information, and model limitations.","visual":"evidence","check":"When should the coach avoid giving overconfident claims?","code":"import torch\n# Compare a narrow assumed distribution with a wider one.\nfor sigma in [3.,10.]:\n    d=torch.distributions.Normal(55.,sigma)\n    print(\"Sigma:\",sigma,\"P(score > 60):\",(1-d.cdf(torch.tensor(60.))).item())\n# Use held-out data to validate uncertainty assumptions.","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. Separate observed variability, imperfect information, and model limitations.","lesson":"predict","checkAnswer":"For example, if only 60% of students in a held-out group predicted at 90% actually pass, the coach has overstated its confidence in that group."},{"section":"08 · PROBABILISTIC PREDICTION","title":"We have one coherent ML pipeline","lead":"Represent → compute → model uncertainty → update → learn → evaluate.","math":"x→μθ(x)→p(y|x;θ)→log-likelihood→evaluation","explain":"The probability refresher becomes preparation for deeper learning algorithms.","visual":"pipeline","check":"Which pieces came from Module 1, and which from Module 2?","code":"import torch\nx = torch.tensor([2., 3.])\nw = torch.tensor([10., 5.]); b = torch.tensor(20.)\nmu = x @ w + b\nmodel = torch.distributions.Normal(mu, torch.tensor(10.))\nprint(mu.item(), (1-model.cdf(torch.tensor(60.))).item())","note":"Use the Campus Study Coach illustration and have learners explain the denominator or assumption. The probability refresher becomes preparation for deeper learning algorithms.","lesson":"predict","checkAnswer":"Module 01 supplied x, w, b and the mean xᵀw + b. Module 02 placed a distribution around that mean and used likelihood to score observed outcomes."},{"section":"SYNTHESIS","title":"The foundations now connect","lead":"Module 1 gave us tensor computation. Module 2 gave us probability distributions, conditional evidence, and likelihood-based fitting.","math":"x → μθ(x) → p(y|x;θ) → −log p(y|x;θ)","visual":"pipeline","check":"Which part of this chain uses matrix multiplication?","note":"Revisit the original score calculation and ask students to explain each additional probabilistic step.","code":"import torch\nx=torch.tensor([2.,3.]);w=torch.tensor([10.,5.])\nmu=x@w+20; d=torch.distributions.Normal(mu,10.)\nprint(\"Prediction:\",mu.item())\nprint(\"Log density of observed score 60:\",d.log_prob(torch.tensor(60.)).item())","checkAnswer":"The prediction step uses X @ w to compute one weighted sum per student, then adds b. For Ava alone the corresponding operation is x @ w."},{"section":"UP NEXT · MODULE 03","title":"Ava scored 60. What should the model change?","lead":"Before the exam, we used 60 as a threshold and asked how likely Ava was to exceed it. Now suppose 60 is her observed score on a fictional exam.","math":"μ = 2(10) + 3(5) + 20 = 55     σ = 10     L = −log p(y = 60 | x) ≈ 3.346524","explain":"Here p is the Gaussian density evaluated at 60. Keep Ava’s two study hours, three practice sets, and the assumed spread fixed. Can we lower this loss by moving the mean?","visual":[["Module 01","55","mean from the weights and bias"],["Module 02","3.346524","Gaussian loss at the observed 60"],["Module 03","Which way?","adjust each weight and the bias"]],"check":"If the mean moved from 55 to 56, would the density at 60 rise or fall? What would happen to the loss?","note":"At fixed spread, moving toward the observation raises its density and lowers the loss. Ask students for the direction first. Open Module 03 to calculate the size of each parameter change.","code":"import torch\n\nx = torch.tensor([2., 3.], dtype=torch.float64)\nw = x.new_tensor([10., 5.])\nb = x.new_tensor(20.)\nmu = x @ w + b\nsigma = x.new_tensor(10.)\ny = x.new_tensor(60.)\n\nfor mean in (mu, mu + 1):\n    loss = -torch.distributions.Normal(mean, sigma).log_prob(y)\n    print(f\"Mean {mean.item():.0f}, observed {y.item():.0f}, NLL {loss.item():.6f}\")","checkAnswer":"The density at 60 rises because 56 is closer to 60 with the same spread. The squared-residual term falls from 25/200 to 16/200, so NLL falls from 3.346524 to 3.301524."}],"exercises":[{"id":"opening-count","lesson":"why","question":"Four fictional scores are 50, 60, 65 and 70. Passing means at least 60. What fraction passed? Enter a decimal.","type":"number","answer":0.75,"options":[],"hint":"Include the score of exactly 60 and divide by the total number of students.","explanation":"Three of the four students passed. 3/4 = 0.75."},{"id":"e01","lesson":"why","question":"Which object is a random variable before Ava takes the exam?","type":"choice","options":["Ava's measured hours","Her eventual exam score Y","The known weight vector","The number 2"],"answer":1,"hint":"Y can take one of several possible values.","explanation":"The model input x is observed; exam score Y is uncertain."},{"id":"e02","lesson":"why","question":"If 18 of 60 observed students passed, what is their empirical pass frequency?","type":"number","options":[],"answer":0.3,"hint":"Divide favorable outcomes by all observed students.","explanation":"18/60 = 0.3."},{"id":"e03","lesson":"describe","question":"A Bernoulli variable has P(Y=1)=0.7. Find P(Y=0).","type":"number","options":[],"answer":0.3,"hint":"Binary outcomes exhaust the sample space.","explanation":"1−0.7=0.3."},{"id":"e04","lesson":"describe","question":"For a continuous Normal variable, which quantity gives P(50≤Y≤60)?","type":"choice","options":["The height f(55)","The area under f from 50 to 60","The value of μ","The standard deviation"],"answer":1,"hint":"Probability on an interval is an area, not a height.","explanation":"Integrate the density over the requested interval."},{"id":"e05","lesson":"moments","question":"A discrete score is 40 or 60, each with probability 0.5. What is E[Y]?","type":"number","options":[],"answer":50,"hint":"Multiply scores by probabilities and add.","explanation":"0.5·40+0.5·60=50."},{"id":"e06","lesson":"moments","question":"Two Normal score models both have μ=55, with σ=5 and σ=15. Which is more concentrated near 55?","type":"choice","options":["σ=15","σ=5","They have identical spreads","Cannot compare"],"answer":1,"hint":"Smaller standard deviation means less spread.","explanation":"The model with σ=5 puts more probability close to the mean."},{"id":"e07","lesson":"conditional","question":"From the 100-student table, what is P(Pass)?","type":"number","options":[],"answer":0.58,"hint":"Count all pass outcomes: 36+22.","explanation":"58/100=0.58."},{"id":"e08","lesson":"conditional","question":"What is P(Pass | Practice≥3) in the table?","type":"number","options":[],"answer":0.8,"hint":"Condition on the 45 students with ≥3 sets.","explanation":"36/(36+9)=0.8."},{"id":"e09","lesson":"bayes","question":"With prior P(Pass)=0.6, P(Strong|Pass)=0.8, P(Strong|Fail)=0.25, compute P(Strong).","type":"number","options":[],"answer":0.58,"hint":"Use the total probability across pass and fail.","explanation":"0.8·0.6+0.25·0.4=0.58."},{"id":"e10","lesson":"bayes","question":"Under the same assumptions, compute P(Pass|Strong), to three decimals.","type":"number","options":[],"answer":0.828,"hint":"Divide the joint P(Pass∩Strong)=0.48 by P(Strong)=0.58.","explanation":"0.48/0.58≈0.8276, rounded to .828."},{"id":"e11","lesson":"families","question":"Choose a distribution for one yes/no pass event.","type":"choice","options":["Gaussian","Bernoulli","Binomial with n=20","Normal"],"answer":1,"hint":"There are exactly two outcomes.","explanation":"Bernoulli models one binary outcome."},{"id":"e12","lesson":"families","question":"Which distribution directly models a continuous exam score?","type":"choice","options":["Categorical","Bernoulli","Gaussian (with assumptions)","Binomial"],"answer":2,"hint":"Think about continuous numerical values.","explanation":"A Gaussian can describe a continuous score approximately."},{"id":"e13","lesson":"learn","question":"For scores [42,48,55,61,69], what is the Gaussian MLE mean?","type":"number","options":[],"answer":55,"hint":"The Gaussian MLE mean is the average.","explanation":"Their sum is 275; divide by 5 to obtain 55."},{"id":"e14","lesson":"learn","question":"For the same data, what is the Gaussian MLE variance (denominator n)?","type":"number","options":[],"answer":90,"hint":"Compute squared deviations: 169+49+0+36+196.","explanation":"The sum 450 divided by 5 gives 90."},{"id":"e15","lesson":"predict","question":"A logistic link converts a real-valued score into what range?","type":"choice","options":["Negative numbers only","Exactly {0,1}","Strictly between 0 and 1","Any real number"],"answer":2,"hint":"Recall σ(z)=1/(1+exp(−z)).","explanation":"A sigmoid output is in (0,1)."},{"id":"e16","lesson":"predict","question":"Which evaluation procedure best checks whether probabilities generalize?","type":"choice","options":["Inspect training accuracy only","Evaluate NLL and calibration on held-out data","Always predict 0.5","Choose the narrowest distribution"],"answer":1,"hint":"Check unseen observations and probability quality.","explanation":"Held-out predictive performance and calibration test predictions beyond training data."}],"faq":[{"id":"m2-faq-01","question":"Why is the predicted score 55 not the student's actual exam score?","answer":"The value 55 comes from multiplying Ava's observed features [2,3] by illustrative weights [10,5] and adding bias 20. It is one numerical output from a toy rule. An eventual score can differ because the recorded inputs do not describe every influence. Probability lets us represent plausible outcomes instead of treating a point estimate as guaranteed.","formal":"ŷ=xᵀw+b=55; Y denotes the uncertain actual outcome.","code":"x=torch.tensor([2.,3.]); print((x@torch.tensor([10.,5.])+20).item())","check":"Name one source of variation not encoded in x.","topic":"why","approved":true,"checkAnswer":"For example, two students with the same hours and number of practice sets may have different prior knowledge."},{"id":"m2-faq-02","question":"What is a random variable?","answer":"A random variable assigns a numerical value to each possible outcome of an uncertain experiment. Before the assessment, Y may represent Ava's eventual score. Once the outcome is observed, that realization is an ordinary known number.","formal":"Y: Ω→ℝ","code":"Y = torch.distributions.Normal(55.,10.).sample()","check":"How is the variable Y different from its observed realization?","topic":"why","approved":true,"checkAnswer":"Y names the uncertain score before the exam. Once Ava scores 60, y = 60 is the observed value."},{"id":"m2-faq-03","question":"What is the difference between an outcome and an event?","answer":"An outcome names one possible result, such as a score of 67. An event collects outcomes, such as all scores of at least 60. We assign probabilities to events. For a discrete variable, a single value may also define an event.","formal":"A={ω∈Ω:Y(ω)≥60}","code":"event = samples >= 60","check":"Which scores belong to the passing event?","topic":"why","approved":true,"checkAnswer":"Every score at least 60 belongs to the passing event, including 60 itself."},{"id":"m2-faq-04","question":"How does empirical frequency differ from a model probability?","answer":"Empirical frequency counts observed cases in a dataset. A model probability describes the proposed process that generates outcomes. The frequency can inform the model but depends on sampling, sample size, and whether the data resemble the target population.","formal":"P̂(A)=count(A)/n","code":"frequency = (observed >= 60).float().mean()","check":"Could a new sample have a different frequency?","topic":"why","approved":true,"checkAnswer":"Yes. Another class sample can have a different number of passes even when the underlying pass probability stays the same."},{"id":"m2-faq-05","question":"What is the difference between PMF and PDF?","answer":"A probability mass function assigns probabilities to individual values of a discrete random variable. A probability density function describes a continuous variable; probabilities are areas across intervals, not the density at one exact point.","formal":"Σᵧp(y)=1 (discrete); ∫f(y)dy=1 (continuous)","code":"d=torch.distributions.Normal(55.,10.); print(d.log_prob(torch.tensor(60.)))","check":"Can a density be greater than one?","topic":"describe","approved":true,"checkAnswer":"Yes. A uniform density on [0, 0.5] has height 2. Its total area is 2 × 0.5 = 1."},{"id":"m2-faq-06","question":"What does the CDF tell us?","answer":"The cumulative distribution function F(t) gives the probability that the random variable is at most t. For a continuous score distribution, the probability of exceeding a threshold is 1−F(t).","formal":"F(t)=P(Y≤t)","code":"d=torch.distributions.Normal(55.,10.); print(d.cdf(torch.tensor(60.)))","check":"How do you compute P(Y>60) from F(60)?","topic":"describe","approved":true,"checkAnswer":"Subtract F(60) from 1. F(60) already includes 60 and all smaller scores."},{"id":"m2-faq-07","question":"Is the value of a PDF at 55 the probability of scoring exactly 55?","answer":"No. For an ideal continuous distribution, the probability of any one exact value is zero. The PDF height measures density. Probabilities come from intervals; real exams may have rounded scores, which can be modeled with discrete bins instead.","formal":"P(50≤Y≤60)=∫₅₀⁶⁰ f(y)dy","code":"d=torch.distributions.Normal(55.,10.); print(d.cdf(torch.tensor(60.))-d.cdf(torch.tensor(50.)))","check":"What interval would represent a rounded score of 55?","topic":"describe","approved":true,"checkAnswer":"When rounding to the nearest integer with half points rounded up, scores from 54.5 inclusive to 55.5 exclusive round to 55. The probability is the area over that interval."},{"id":"m2-faq-08","question":"What does expectation mean?","answer":"Expectation is a probability-weighted average over possible outcomes. It is the center of a distribution in the sense of long-run average under standard assumptions, not a promise about one observation.","formal":"E[Y]=Σy p(y), or ∫y f(y)dy","code":"values=torch.tensor([40.,60.]); probs=torch.tensor([.5,.5]); print((values*probs).sum())","check":"Can expectation be a value that never occurs?","topic":"moments","approved":true,"checkAnswer":"Yes. A fair 0/1 outcome has expectation 0.5, although a single trial can only produce 0 or 1."},{"id":"m2-faq-09","question":"Why square deviations when calculating variance?","answer":"The average signed deviation from the mean is zero, so it would not measure spread. Squaring deviations gives positive contributions; the square root converts variance into a standard deviation expressed in the original units.","formal":"Var(Y)=E[(Y−E[Y])²]","code":"x=torch.tensor([42.,48.,55.,61.,69.]); print(((x-x.mean())**2).mean())","check":"Why is a standard deviation easier to interpret than variance?","topic":"moments","approved":true,"checkAnswer":"Standard deviation uses the original score units. A spread of 10 points is easier to compare with exam scores than a variance of 100 squared points."},{"id":"m2-faq-10","question":"Why does a broader distribution matter if the mean stays 55?","answer":"A wider distribution places more probability on outcomes far from 55. Thus two models can have the same expected score but give different probability for scoring above 60 or staying close to the mean.","formal":"Normal(55,5²) ≠ Normal(55,15²)","code":"for s in [5.,15.]: print(s,(1-torch.distributions.Normal(55.,s).cdf(torch.tensor(60.))).item())","check":"Which model has more extreme outcomes?","topic":"moments","approved":true,"checkAnswer":"With equal Gaussian means, the larger σ gives a greater probability of scores more than a fixed distance from the mean."},{"id":"m2-faq-11","question":"What is the difference between joint and conditional probability?","answer":"Joint probability concerns both events and uses the entire population as reference. Conditional probability restricts attention to cases where the conditioning event occurs. In our toy table, 36/100 describes passing and practicing; 36/45 describes passing among practice-group students.","formal":"P(A|B)=P(A∩B)/P(B)","code":"joint=36/100; conditional=36/45; print(joint,conditional)","check":"Why are these denominators different?","topic":"conditional","approved":true,"checkAnswer":"A joint frequency divides by all 100 students. A conditional frequency divides only by the students who satisfy the condition."},{"id":"m2-faq-12","question":"Why is P(Pass|Practice) not P(Practice|Pass)?","answer":"The first asks how many practice-group students passed, using all practice-group students as denominator. The second asks how many passing students practiced, using all passing students as denominator. In our example they are 36/45 and 36/58.","formal":"P(A|B)≠P(B|A) generally","code":"print(36/45,36/58)","check":"Which denominator would you use if you start with the passing students?","topic":"conditional","approved":true,"checkAnswer":"Use 58, the total who passed. Then 36/58 is the fraction of passing students who practiced at least three sets."},{"id":"m2-faq-13","question":"Does association imply that practice causes passing?","answer":"No. Even if P(Pass|Practice) is higher than the overall passing rate, prior preparation, motivation, or other unobserved factors can explain some association. The toy table illustrates conditional probability, not a causal study.","formal":"P(A|B)≠P(A) does not establish B→A causally.","code":"counts=torch.tensor([[36.,9.],[22.,33.]])","check":"What would you change to study causal effects?","topic":"conditional","approved":true,"checkAnswer":"For example, randomly assign different practice schedules and compare later scores across groups using the same exam and teaching conditions."},{"id":"m2-faq-14","question":"What is a prior in Bayes' rule?","answer":"The prior represents our probability for a hypothesis before observing a particular new signal. It may come from previous data or modeling assumptions. Updating the prior with new evidence produces the posterior.","formal":"P(H|E)∝P(E|H)P(H)","code":"prior=torch.tensor([.6,.4])","check":"What evidence was available before the new practice test?","topic":"bayes","approved":true,"checkAnswer":"Before the signal, the example used a 60% baseline pass probability. The student’s new practice-test result enters in the update."},{"id":"m2-faq-15","question":"What is the likelihood in a Bayesian update?","answer":"The likelihood describes how probable the observed evidence is under each proposed hypothesis. It is not the posterior probability of the hypothesis. In our example, a strong test is seen with probability 0.8 when a student passes and 0.25 when they fail.","formal":"P(E|H), not P(H|E)","code":"likelihood=torch.tensor([.8,.25])","check":"What does the second likelihood value represent?","topic":"bayes","approved":true,"checkAnswer":"0.25 is P(Strong | Fail). Among students who eventually fail, one quarter receive a strong practice-test signal in this example."},{"id":"m2-faq-16","question":"Why do we divide by total evidence probability?","answer":"Multiplying prior and likelihood gives unnormalized weights for each hypothesis. Dividing by their sum makes the resulting posterior probabilities sum to one, producing a valid distribution.","formal":"P(H|E)=P(E|H)P(H)/P(E)","code":"w=torch.tensor([.6,.4])*torch.tensor([.8,.25]); print(w/w.sum())","check":"What happens if you forget to normalize?","topic":"bayes","approved":true,"checkAnswer":"The two weights here are 0.48 and 0.10. They sum to 0.58, the probability of the evidence. Dividing each by 0.58 turns them into posterior probabilities."},{"id":"m2-faq-17","question":"When should I use Bernoulli rather than a Normal distribution?","answer":"Use Bernoulli for one binary outcome, such as pass/fail. Use Normal when a continuous measurement is reasonably described by a bell-shaped model. The support of the variable and assumptions must match the task.","formal":"Y∈{0,1} versus Y∈ℝ","code":"b=torch.distributions.Bernoulli(probs=.8); n=torch.distributions.Normal(55.,10.)","check":"Does a Normal distribution assign mass only to whole exam scores?","topic":"families","approved":true,"checkAnswer":"No. A Normal model is continuous and assigns density to fractional scores as well. Use a Bernoulli distribution for one binary result."},{"id":"m2-faq-18","question":"What is the difference between Bernoulli and Binomial?","answer":"Bernoulli represents one binary trial. Binomial counts successes across a fixed number of trials, under assumptions such as independence and the same success probability for each trial.","formal":"Bernoulli(p); Binomial(n,p)","code":"b=torch.distributions.Binomial(total_count=10,probs=.8); print(b.mean)","check":"What additional assumptions does Binomial need?","topic":"families","approved":true,"checkAnswer":"A binomial count assumes a fixed number of independent Bernoulli trials, each with the same success probability."},{"id":"m2-faq-19","question":"Why is a categorical distribution different from a regression score?","answer":"A categorical model assigns probabilities to a finite set of labels, such as low, medium and high. A regression score is numerical and its order and distance generally matter. The two representations answer different kinds of questions.","formal":"P(Y=k)=pₖ, Σₖpₖ=1","code":"c=torch.distributions.Categorical(probs=torch.tensor([.2,.5,.3])); print(c.sample())","check":"Why can't a class label be interpreted as 20 score points?","topic":"families","approved":true,"checkAnswer":"A category number identifies a group. Relabeling that group changes the number without changing its meaning, so its numeric value cannot be read as exam points."},{"id":"m2-faq-20","question":"What does likelihood optimize?","answer":"For fixed observed data, likelihood evaluates how different parameter settings explain the observations. Maximum likelihood chooses parameters maximizing the joint density or mass under an assumed model.","formal":"θ̂=argmaxθ Σᵢ log p(yᵢ|θ)","code":"y=torch.tensor([42.,48.,55.,61.,69.]); print(y.mean())","check":"What is held fixed while parameters are varied?","topic":"learn","approved":true,"checkAnswer":"Keep the observations fixed and vary the proposed model parameters."},{"id":"m2-faq-21","question":"Why use log-likelihood?","answer":"Many likelihood terms multiplied together can become extremely small. Logarithms turn the product into a sum; because log is increasing, the maximizing parameter choice remains the same.","formal":"log ∏ᵢpᵢ = Σᵢlog pᵢ","code":"dist=torch.distributions.Normal(55.,10.); print(dist.log_prob(torch.tensor([42.,48.,55.,61.,69.])).sum())","check":"Which numerical problem does using logs reduce?","topic":"learn","approved":true,"checkAnswer":"Multiplying many tiny likelihood values can underflow to zero. Adding their log values makes the calculation numerically manageable."},{"id":"m2-faq-22","question":"Why is the Gaussian MLE variance divided by n?","answer":"Maximizing the Gaussian likelihood for a dataset produces an estimate with denominator n. The familiar unbiased sample variance uses n−1 for an unbiased estimate under independent sampling; the two quantities optimize different objectives.","formal":"σ̂²_MLE=Σᵢ(yᵢ−ȳ)²/n","code":"y=torch.tensor([42.,48.,55.,61.,69.]); print(y.var(unbiased=False),y.var(unbiased=True))","check":"Which estimator corresponds to maximum likelihood?","topic":"learn","approved":true,"checkAnswer":"The Gaussian maximum-likelihood estimate is the sum of squared deviations from the fitted mean divided by n."},{"id":"m2-faq-23","question":"Can a point model become a probabilistic predictor?","answer":"Yes. The tensor model can determine a conditional mean μθ(x), and a distribution can describe variation around it. This requires choosing or learning a spread and then checking the resulting probabilities against data.","formal":"Y|X=x∼Normal(μθ(x),σθ(x)²)","code":"mu=x@w+b; d=torch.distributions.Normal(mu,torch.tensor(10.))","check":"Who provides the distribution's σ?","topic":"predict","approved":true,"checkAnswer":"The modeler can specify σ, as we do with 10 points, or fit it using observed residuals."},{"id":"m2-faq-24","question":"Why evaluate calibration as well as accuracy?","answer":"A predictor can classify many cases correctly yet be overconfident. Calibration asks whether events predicted at about 70% frequency occur about 70% of the time across appropriate groups of held-out predictions.","formal":"Calibration: P(Y=1 | predicted probability≈p)≈p","code":"# Evaluate probability bins and outcome rates on held-out data","check":"What pattern signals overconfidence?","topic":"predict","approved":true,"checkAnswer":"If students assigned a 90% chance of passing actually pass substantially less often across enough comparable cases, the model is overconfident in that group."}]};
